reviews.remaining:
**The middle tier does not exist.** The row asks for three stacked layers; this is the first, with its matching repaired (and repaired again in PR #1177). There is still no ~40-rule severity/reversibility-tagged risky-pattern catalogue feeding the approval card's description, so `rm -rf ~/projects` is neither hardline nor pattern-classified — re-checked 2026-09-19 on 494a13b2: `hardline_match("rm -rf ~/projects")` and `hardline_match(['rm','-rf','/home/x/projects'])` both return None, and the module carries no severity/reversibility/catalogue vocabulary at all — and the owner's card still says "run this argv" rather than "recursive delete", which the row's rationale names as the point. No user-editable `approvals.deny` fnmatch list at hardline level (no `fnmatch`/`deny` in the module). No parser limits that themselves hardline-block and save an oversized payload to a file for manual review (128k chars / 4096 separator-free / 25k segments); `MAX_ARG_CHARS = 4000` is argv validation, not a refusal. **Variants Hermes produces and this still does not:** NFKC normalisation, `$HOME`/`$HERMES_HOME` folding, grep-pattern span masking, and `python -c` / `perl -e` / `node -e` payload extraction — the last left alone on purpose, since `jobs_scripts.snapshot_script` legitimately builds `[sys.executable, '-I', '-c', source]`. `$()`/backtick substitution is now extracted in ONE place only: #1177's `_substituted` narrows a heredoc BODY line to its substitutions, so `cat <<EOF` / `$(rm -rf /)` / `EOF` refuses; an ordinary line is still not expanded, so `echo $(rm -rf /)` and a backtick `rm -rf /` inside an assignment return None (a `$(mkfs.ext4 …)` is caught only through `_CMD`'s `(` anchor). The row's own examples `$(printf mkfs).ext4 /dev/sda` and `echo bWtmcw==|base64 -d|sh` still return None. **Gaps this module knows it still has, listed honestly rather than as a complete set:** nesting deeper than `_MAX_SHELL_DEPTH` (depth-3 `sh -c "sh -c 'sh -c …'"` returns None, pinned by test); a quote at a word BOUNDARY (`'mkfs.ext4' /dev/sda`) rather than inside a word; `powershell -EncodedCommand` base64; a `cmd /c` payload split across several argv items; an obfuscated shell HEAD in argv form (`['s""h','-c',…]`), because the de-obfuscation pass runs after payload extraction and its output is not fed back through it; and, since #1177, two in the false-refusal direction — a `case` label with a leading `(` (`(shutdown) echo bye ;;`) is still read as a command, and a `case` nested inside another is not modelled: its inner first label IS stripped (`case $1 in
a) case $2 in
reboot) echo r ;;
esac ;;
esac` went power_cycle→None), so the docstring's 'not handled' is about the region boundary, not a guaranteed refusal. **Refusals #1177 cleared in the dangerous direction (not yet closed, no test pins them):** a `case`-label strip that reaches a subshell's last word after an `in` token outside a real case statement (`(echo case in; reboot); echo esac`, and `$IFS`-spliced `mkfs.ext4$IFS/dev/sda` / `wipefs$IFS-a$IFS/dev/sda` inside the same shape); a heredoc body consumed by a shell placed AFTER the redirection (`cat <<EOF | sh`, `cat <<EOF | bash`, `cat <<'EOF' >x.sh; sh x.sh`) — `_heredoc_feeds_a_shell` inspects only the stage left of `<<`. **Two pre-existing false positives are unchanged** (`git commit -m "fix;mkfs.ext4 handling"` → mkfs; `grep -r "(shutdown" docs/` → power_cycle), and `_PATH_PREFIX` added a third class no approval can lift: a path to a script whose basename starts with a hardline name — `./mkfs.sh` (mkfs), `./reboot.sh`, `./scripts/shutdown.sh --dry-run`, `/opt/app/bin/halt` (power_cycle) — all None on 1c5054ea, all refused on 494a13b2 (bare `reboot.sh` already refused before; the prefix extends it to every path spelling). **And a measured cost, re-measured after #1177 removed the breadth cap** (`_MAX_SHELL_EXPANSIONS` and the `MAX_ARG_CHARS` re-expansion guard are gone; every level-1 payload is unwrapped again): on a maximal 64x4000-character argv the pass is roughly unchanged (79.6 ms on the pre-#1177 module vs 82.3 ms after, same machine, best of 5), but a command made of 2,000 nested `sh -c "sh -c 'echo ok'"` segments (57k chars) goes from 196 ms to 380 ms — synchronous, on the event loop, before every exec. The docstring's linear-in-input argument holds (each level's payloads are disjoint substrings); an independent re-run on another machine saw 79.0→80.6 ms and 125→151 ms, so the constant grows for nested input but the factor is machine-dependent and no test pins either number. **Adversarial review of the original slice found four more defects, all fixed and each reproduced first** against real /bin/sh, /bin/dash and /bin/bash: (1) `_shell_c_payloads` demanded the word after the wrappers BE the shell, so `env FOO=1 sh -c 'mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda'` — and `sudo -u root sh -c …`, `nice -n 10 sh -c …` — skipped the whole `sh -c` screening while deleting `FOO=1 ` made the same string `hardline_denied:mkfs`; `_CMD` had been widened for that assignment prefix and the unwrapper had not. (2) `_unquoted_lines` read a backslash-newline as a statement separator when it is a line CONTINUATION, so `mk\` + newline + `fs.ext4 /dev/sda`, which all three shells splice into `mkfs.ext4`, was seen as two harmless fragments. (3) every heredoc body line became a command position, so `cat > x.yaml <<'EOF'` / `shutdown: graceful` / `EOF` was refused as `power_cycle`; body lines were joined as data in the flat text. **#1177's second review showed that fix was incomplete in three ways, all closed there:** `segments` still tokenised every body line, so `cat > README.md <<'EOF'` / `rm -rf / will destroy the box` / `EOF` stayed a `recursive_root_removal` refusal; the rule had no exception for a heredoc a SHELL consumes, so `bash <<EOF` / `mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda` / `EOF` returned None (a bypass the heredoc work itself opened); and any `<<` — inside quotes, inside `$(( ))`, a `<<<` here-string, or with a delimiter that never returns — opened a heredoc and switched the newline screening off for every following line. (4) `{` anchored bare, so `jq '{reboot: .needs_reboot}'` was a `power_cycle` refusal; POSIX requires whitespace after `{` for a brace group, and `{ mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda; }` is still refused. Each fix is red-proofed on its own.
